\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Ordered exterior operators span the endomorphisms}
\date{2026-11-08}

\p{On every square torus the kernel of #{\extd+\extdag} has dimension
#{4}, by the [Hodge theorem](fdip-0090). For periods at least #{3},
the [centered Dirac operator](fdip-000T) has kernel dimension
#{2^{2+e}}. That dimension equals #{4} when both periods are odd,
and it is strictly larger when either period is even.}

\p{Let #{V} be a finite free module of positive rank over a
commutative ring. Exterior multiplication by a vector #{v} is the
module endomorphism #{v\wedge} of the exterior algebra given by
#{(v\wedge)(\omega)=v\wedge\omega}. Contraction by a covector
#{\alpha} is the unique antiderivation of degree #{-1} that
vanishes on #{1} and sends a vector #{v} to #{\alpha(v)}.
The normal-order map sends a pure tensor #{u\otimes\varphi} in
#{\bigwedge V\otimes\bigwedge V^*} to wedge by #{u} after that
contraction by #{\varphi}. On an ordered basis, composing
contractions in increasing index order with the largest index
acting first recovers the operators #{M_{S,T}} of the next
paragraph. The isomorphism below is that coordinate form of the
normal-order map.}

\p{Let #{k} be a commutative ring and let #{n} be a positive integer.
Write #{\bigwedge} for the exterior algebra of the free module on
basis vectors #{e_1,\ldots,e_n}, with basis the monomials #{e_I}
indexed by subsets #{I}, written in increasing order, and with
#{e_\varnothing=1}. Exterior multiplication and contraction along
the basis are the linear operators
##{(e_i\wedge)(e_I)=e_i\wedge e_I,}
##{(e_i\cdot)(e_I)=0}
when #{i\notin I}. When #{i\in I}, let
#{p=\lvert\{j\in I:j<i\}\rvert}
and set
##{(e_i\cdot)(e_I)=(-1)^p e_{I\setminus\{i\}}.}
The singleton has #{p=0}, so #{(e_i\cdot)(e_i)=1}. For the factor
in place #{j}, counted from one, #{p=j-1}. That is the exponent
of #{(-1)^{j-1}} in the ordered contraction of \ref{fdip-000P}.
For subsets #{S} and #{T}, write #{(e\wedge)_S} and #{(e\cdot)_T}
for the compositions of these operators in increasing index order,
the largest index acting first, and write
#{M_{S,T}=(e\wedge)_S\circ(e\cdot)_T}. There are #{2^n} choices for
each subset, hence #{4^n} such operators, and they are a basis
of the endomorphism ring of #{\bigwedge}. In particular
#{e_i\wedge+e_i\cdot} satisfies
##{(e_i\wedge+e_i\cdot)(e_j\wedge+e_j\cdot)
 +(e_j\wedge+e_j\cdot)(e_i\wedge+e_i\cdot)
 =2\delta_{ij}\bbI.}
The [configuration Clifford algebra](fdip-000P) is the square
quotient of a supplied quadratic form. The operators here are
defined before any such form, and the displayed relation is the
square relation of the standard dot product. The note does not
claim that the resulting Clifford representation is injective.}

\proof{
\p{The dimension #{4} is the kernel dimension in the Hodge theorem.
The centered count is the cited formula at #{n=2}. It equals #{4}
when #{e=0}. When #{e\ge 1} the factor #{2^e} makes it strictly
larger. The two statements have the scopes already named.}

\p{The monomials are a basis, so operator identities may be checked
on #{e_I}. If #{i\in I}, then #{e_i\wedge e_I=0}. If #{i\notin I}
and #{s} indices of #{I} are smaller than #{i}, then
#{e_i\wedge e_I=(-1)^s e_{I\cup\{i\}}}. Thus #{(e_i\wedge)^2=0}.
For #{i\ne j} the wedge of the two basis vectors changes sign when
their order is exchanged, so
#{(e_i\wedge)(e_j\wedge)+(e_j\wedge)(e_i\wedge)=0}. A monomial contains
#{i} at most once, so #{(e_i\cdot)^2=0}.}

\p{For the contractions, take #{i\ne j} and suppose both lie in
#{I}. Let #{p} be the number of indices of #{I} smaller than #{i},
and let #{r} be the number smaller than #{j}. Order the labels
so that #{i<j}.
Removing #{j} first contributes #{(-1)^r}, and the count for #{i}
is unchanged, so the second removal contributes #{(-1)^p}.
Removing #{i} first contributes #{(-1)^p}. The remaining set has
count #{r-1} below #{j}, so the second removal contributes
#{(-1)^{r-1}}. Both orders remove #{i} with sign #{(-1)^p}. The
two signs for removing #{j} differ by the factor #{-1}. The
products therefore differ by #{-1}, the remaining monomial is the
same, and the sum is zero.
If either index is missing, both double contractions vanish. Hence
#{(e_i\cdot)(e_j\cdot)+(e_j\cdot)(e_i\cdot)=0}.}

\p{Now compare #{(e_j\cdot)(e_i\wedge)} with #{(e_i\wedge)(e_j\cdot)}. If
#{i\ne j} and #{j\notin I}, the contraction of the input is zero.
Exterior multiplication either vanishes or produces a monomial that
still omits #{j}, so the later contraction vanishes as well. If
#{j\in I} and #{i\in I}, exterior multiplication of the input
vanishes, and the contraction still leaves #{i} in place, so the
later exterior multiplication vanishes. The remaining pattern is
#{j\in I} and #{i\notin I}. Let #{p} be the number of indices of
#{I} smaller than #{j}, and let #{s} be the number smaller than
#{i}. The wedge #{e_i\wedge e_I} contributes #{(-1)^s}, and removal
of #{j} from #{I} contributes #{(-1)^p}. If #{i<j}, removal of
#{j} from #{I\cup\{i\}} contributes #{(-1)^{p+1}}, while the wedge
of #{e_i} into #{I\setminus\{j\}} still contributes #{(-1)^s}. If
#{i>j}, removal of #{j} from #{I\cup\{i\}} still contributes
#{(-1)^p}, while the wedge into #{I\setminus\{j\}} contributes
#{(-1)^{s-1}}, because #{j} was counted by #{s}. In both subcases
the two compositions land on the same monomial. The product of
the signs in one order is the product in the other order
multiplied by #{-1}, so the sum is zero. If #{i=j} and
#{i\notin I}, the contraction of the input vanishes. Insertion
uses the count #{s}, and removal of that inserted factor uses the
same count, so #{(e_i\cdot)(e_i\wedge)} returns #{e_I} and the product
of the signs is #{1}. If #{i\in I}, exterior multiplication of the
input vanishes. Removal uses the count #{p}, and the wedge that
restores #{i} uses the same count, so #{(e_i\wedge)(e_i\cdot)} returns
#{e_I} and the product of the signs is #{1}. Therefore
#{(e_i\wedge)(e_j\cdot)+(e_j\cdot)(e_i\wedge)=\delta_{ij}\bbI}.}

\p{The square of #{(e_i\wedge+e_i\cdot)} is
#{(e_i\wedge)(e_i\cdot)+(e_i\cdot)(e_i\wedge)=\bbI}. For #{i\ne j} each of
the 4 pairs in the expansion of
#{(e_i\wedge+e_i\cdot)(e_j\wedge+e_j\cdot)+(e_j\wedge+e_j\cdot)(e_i\wedge+e_i\cdot)} anticommutes, so the sum is
zero. This is the displayed Clifford relation.}

\p{The monomials are a free basis, so the endomorphism ring is
free on the operators that send one monomial to one monomial and
kill the rest. Order the pairs of subsets so that the cardinality
of #{T} increases, and index both the operators #{M_{S,T}} and
these monomial matrix units by that order. The coefficient of
#{e_S} in #{M_{S',T'}e_T} can be nonzero only when
#{T'\subseteq T}. A column with a strictly larger second set
therefore does not meet this row. When the cardinalities are
equal, necessarily #{T'=T}. The contraction #{(e\cdot)_T} removes
every factor of #{e_T}. Each removal meets a present factor, so
the image of the contraction is #{(\pm 1)\,1}. The following
exterior product inserts the factors in #{S'}, each into a
monomial that does not yet contain it, and the image is
#{(\pm 1)\,e_{S'}}. The coefficient of #{e_S} is therefore zero
unless #{S'=S}, in which case it is #{\pm 1}. Within one
cardinality the block is diagonal. A strictly smaller #{T'}
meets the row in an earlier column. The matrix is consequently
lower triangular, with units #{\pm 1} on the diagonal. Given a
target endomorphism, its coefficients against the #{M_{S,T}}
are the unique solution of that system: start from the empty
second set, multiply by the diagonal entry #{\pm 1}, whose square
is #{1}, and at each later row use the coefficients already found.
The operators are therefore a basis.}
}

\p{When #{n=1} the four operators are the identity, exterior
multiplication by the basis vector, contraction by its dual
coordinate, and their composite. The triangular argument already
shows that these four operators span the endomorphism module and
are independent over the commutative ring, so they are a basis.
That is the case #{4^n=4} of the count above. The conclusion is
this spanning-and-independence statement. It does not claim that
every four independent endomorphisms of a rank-two module form a
basis.}
